cards.body:
Because `int *p;` creates only a pointer variable, not an `int` for it to point at.

Until you assign an address to `p`, it holds a garbage value (if it is a local variable). Writing `*p = 5;` then stores 5 at some random address, which is undefined behavior: a crash at best, silent corruption at worst.

Example: Correct use gives the pointer something real to point at first.

```c
int n;
int *p = &n;
*p = 5;
```

Tip: Initialize every pointer when you declare it, to the address of a real object or to `NULL`.

