cards.body:
A type that groups several named members, possibly of different types, into one block of memory.

Members are stored in the order you declare them. You declare the type once, then make variables of it.

Example:

```c
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};
struct Point p = { 3, 4 };
```

Why it matters: A struct lets you pass a sensor reading, a packet header or a configuration as one object, instead of a pile of loose variables.

