cards.body:
If `realloc` fails it returns `NULL` and leaves the old block allocated, so overwriting `a` loses the only pointer to it and leaks it. Use a temporary.

```c
int *tmp = realloc(a, new_size);
if (tmp == NULL) {
    free(a);
    return -1;
}
a = tmp;
```

`realloc(p, n)` resizes a block to `n` bytes, keeping the old contents up to the smaller size. It may move the block to a new address, so always use the pointer it returns.

Watch out: After a successful `realloc`, any other pointer into the old block may be dangling.

